# Koha systemd units

This package supervises every Koha daemon with native systemd units. It is
one of the two providers of the `koha-init` virtual package that koha-common
depends on; the other is koha-sysv (the traditional /etc/init.d/koha-common
script). The two conflict with each other and installing one replaces the
other. koha-common prefers koha-systemd wherever systemd is installable, so
containers that want the init script must install koha-sysv explicitly.

## Units

Per instance (`INSTANCE` is the koha-create name, e.g. `library`):

- `koha-plack@INSTANCE.service`             Plack/Starman web application
- `koha-zebra@INSTANCE.service`             Zebra search server
- `koha-indexer@INSTANCE.service`           Zebra indexing daemon
- `koha-es-indexer@INSTANCE.service`        Elasticsearch indexing daemon
- `koha-worker@INSTANCE.service`            background jobs, `default` queue
- `koha-worker-long_tasks@INSTANCE.service` background jobs, `long_tasks` queue
- `koha-sip@INSTANCE.service`               SIP2 server
- `koha-z3950@INSTANCE.service`             Z39.50/SRU server
- `koha-create-dirs@INSTANCE.service`       oneshot that creates the run,
                                            lock and cache directories the
                                            others depend on
- `koha@INSTANCE.target`                    groups the instance's units

Host wide:

- `koha.target`          groups every `koha@INSTANCE.target`; enabled at boot
- `koha-common.service`  compatibility shim so that
                         `systemctl start|stop|restart koha-common` (the old
                         `service koha-common ...`) keeps working. It has no
                         state of its own: `systemctl status koha.target`, or
                         `koha-systemd-ctl status INSTANCE`, is the real
                         health check.

## Day-to-day use

The koha-* helpers work as before and drive systemd underneath:

    koha-plack --restart library         # systemctl restart koha-plack@library
    koha-zebra --stop library
    koha-worker --all-queues --status library

Each helper also has `--enable` and `--disable`, which control whether the
unit is started at boot (with koha-sysv these are no-ops). Or use systemctl
directly:

    systemctl restart koha@library.target       # one instance, all daemons
    systemctl restart koha-common               # every instance
    systemctl status koha-plack@library.service
    koha-systemd-ctl status library             # all units of one instance

koha-create enables and starts the units of a new instance. koha-disable
stops an instance and disables its `koha@INSTANCE.target`, koha-enable
turns it back on and starts it, and koha-remove disables everything it
created.

Which units are enabled follows the instance's configuration:

- Plack: `koha-plack --enable INSTANCE` (also edits the Apache vhost)
- SIP: `koha-sip --enable INSTANCE`. The SysV-era flag file
  /var/lib/koha/INSTANCE/sip.enabled is not consulted on systemd hosts;
  `systemctl is-enabled koha-sip@INSTANCE` is the source of truth.
- Z39.50/SRU: `koha-z3950-responder --enable INSTANCE`
- Zebra indexer: enabled when USE_INDEXER_DAEMON=yes in
  /etc/default/koha-common (the default)
- Elasticsearch indexer: `koha-es-indexer --enable INSTANCE`. Switching the
  SearchEngine system preference does not enable anything by itself;
  `systemctl restart koha-common` (or `koha-systemd-ctl sync-es-indexers`)
  discovers instances that now use Elasticsearch and enables their indexer,
  like the init script did on every start.

## Configuration

The units read the same settings as the init script:

- /etc/koha/sites/INSTANCE/koha-conf.xml: plack_workers,
  plack_max_requests, debug_mode, zebra_loglevels, zebra_max_record_size,
  es_indexer_batch_size, and z3950_responder_options in the Z39.50
  config.xml
- /etc/default/koha-common: INDEXER_TIMEOUT, ALTERNATE_INDEXER_DAEMON,
  INDEXER_PARAMS, Z3950_ADDITIONAL_OPTS

They are re-read on every start by koha-generate-env, which writes them to
/run/koha/INSTANCE/SERVICE.env for the unit's EnvironmentFile=. Restart the
unit after changing them.

## Logging

Log locations are the same with either init package. With koha-sysv the
daemon(1) wrapper captures each daemon's standard output and error to
/var/log/koha/INSTANCE/NAME-output.log and NAME-error.log; the systemd
units do the same through StandardOutput=append: and StandardError=append:,
so zebra-*.log, indexer-*.log, es-indexer-*.log, worker-*.log and
sip-*.log keep being written where they always were and the shipped
logrotate configuration keeps rotating them. Everything Koha writes itself
is unaffected either way:

    plack.log, plack-error.log          (Starman --access-log/--error-log)
    plack-opac-error.log, plack-intranet-error.log, plack-api-error.log,
    api-error.log, sip-output.log, z3950-error.log, editrace.log
                                        (log4perl)
    z3950.log                            (z3950_responder.pl -l)
    opac-error.log, intranet-error.log, opac-access.log, ...
                                        (Apache, per vhost)

The journal therefore only holds what systemd itself records about a unit
(starts, stops, failures, restart-limit hits), which is where to look when
a unit refuses to start:

    journalctl -u koha-zebra@library.service
    journalctl -u 'koha-*@library.service'    # everything for one instance

If you would rather have a daemon's output in the journal, override the two
directives with a drop-in:

    systemctl edit koha-worker@library.service
    [Service]
    StandardOutput=journal
    StandardError=journal

## Sandboxing

The daemon units run with ProtectSystem=full, ProtectHome=yes,
PrivateTmp=yes and NoNewPrivileges=yes. This is fine for instances created
by koha-create, which keep everything under /var/lib/koha/INSTANCE,
/var/cache/koha/INSTANCE and /var/log/koha/INSTANCE. It matters if:

- koha-conf.xml has an empty <tmp_path> (instances created before that
  option existed): Koha then falls back to /tmp, which is private to each
  unit, so file-based sessions do not survive a Plack restart and are not
  shared with CGI, and uploads staged by Plack are invisible to the worker.
  Set <tmp_path>/var/lib/koha/INSTANCE/tmp</tmp_path> (and create it) as
  koha-create does today.
- --upload-path, --tmp-path or a plugin directory point under /home
  (ProtectHome) or anywhere outside the instance directories (ProtectSystem
  makes /usr, /boot and /etc read-only).
- a plugin shells out to a setuid helper such as sudo (NoNewPrivileges).

Relax a setting for one unit with a drop-in rather than editing the
packaged file:

    systemctl edit koha-plack@library.service
    [Service]
    PrivateTmp=no
    ReadWritePaths=/srv/koha-uploads

## Migration from koha-sysv / the old koha-common

Installing koha-systemd (which happens automatically when upgrading
koha-common from a pre-split release on a systemd host) stops the daemons
the init script started, enables the units matching each enabled
instance's configuration, and starts koha.target. No manual step is
needed. To go back, `apt install koha-sysv`: koha-systemd is removed, its
prerm stops the units, and the init script takes over on the next start.
